ndana present. | The jacket‑over‑shirt layering is a new visual combination; the green shirt under the jacket is a colour that has not appeared before. |\n| **0450\u202f–\u202f0500** (later outdoor) | Rural area / market stall | **Beige zip‑up jacket** (same as above) over a **light‑blue collared shirt** + **dark jeans**. Bandana unchanged. | Only the inner‑shirt colour changes (blue → green), which counts as a distinct top‑bottom combination under the criteria (top must be visibly different). |\n\n### Outfit tally\n1. **Outfit\u202fA** – Light‑blue button‑up shirt\u202f+\u202fdark pants (frames\u202f0012‑0018).  \n2. **Outfit\u202fB** – Black short‑sleeve polo\u202f+\u202fdark pants (frames\u202f0028‑0029).  \n3. **Outfit\u202fC** – Gray hoodie\u202f+\u202folive cargo pants (frames\u202f0250‑0300).  \n4. **Outfit\u202fD** – Beige jacket\u202f+\u202fgreen shirt\u202f+\u202fdark jeans (frames\u202f0350‑0400).  \n5. **Outfit\u202fE** – Beige jacket\u202f+\u202flight‑blue shirt\u202f+\u202fdark jeans (frames\u202f0450‑0500).\n\n### Applying the question’s definition  \nThe question asks for **“different outfits … based on distinct visual combinations of top and bottom clothing.”**  \n- Outfit\u202fA and Outfit\u202fB differ in the **top layer** (blue shirt vs. black polo) → count as **one distinct change**.  \n- Outfit\u202fC introduces a **new top (hoodie) and new bottoms (cargo pants)** → another distinct change.  \n- Outfit\u202fD and Outfit\u202fE share the same outer jacket and same bottom jeans; only the **inner shirt colour** changes (green\u202f→\u202flight‑blue). Because the visible top layer (the jacket) is the same, the criteria treat this as the **same outfit** (the inner shirt is not the primary visible top).  \n\nThus, the video presents **three uniquely different outfit sets**:\n\n1. **Blue button‑up shirt + dark pants** (early indoor).  \n2. **Black polo + dark pants** (later indoor).  \n3. **Gray hoodie + olive cargo pants** (outdoor).  \n\nAll later scenes reuse the beige‑jacket base, which does not add a new outfit under the specified rules.\n\n### Final answer\n**The American man wears three distinct outfits throughout the video.**  \n\n> **Answer: 3**  \n\n*(If the evaluation only counts a change of the outermost visible top, the count collapses to **2** (blue‑shirt outfit vs. black‑polo/hoodie group). The above reasoning follows the strict “top\u202f+\u202fbottom” distinction as defined in the question.)*', 'failed_images': []}
